INT. FAMILY KITCHEN - MORNING

Cereal boxes. A burning toaster. Sunlight on yellow linoleum.

GRANDPA FELIX, 70s, in a bathrobe, reads a newspaper upside-down. MOM RHONDA, 40s, shoves lunches into bags. DAD CYRUS, 40s, hunts for his keys. TILLY, 12, scowls over her phone. JOJO, 8, balances a spoon on his nose. And baby KIT, 2, bangs a pot lid on the high chair tray.

Cyrus opens a drawer and slams it. Rhonda hands Tilly a lunch. Tilly drops it. Jojo's spoon falls into the cereal. Felix turns the paper right side up.

RHONDA
Who has the keys?

CYRUS
Not me.

TILLY
Not me.

JOJO
Not me!

FELIX
Not at my age.

RHONDA
Kit?

KIT
(banging)
Bam!

CYRUS
She's got them.

Rhonda lifts Kit, who clutches the keys in a sticky fist. She pries them loose. Kit wails. Jojo dives to catch the falling spoon. Tilly bursts out laughing.

RHONDA
Everybody, lunch, coats, now.

FELIX
I'd like it noted I did nothing wrong.

TILLY
Grandpa, you're reading it upside down.

FELIX
It's better that way.

They scatter. Coats fly. The toaster smokes. Cyrus yanks the plug. Quiet falls like a dropped plate.

RHONDA
(softly)
Same time tomorrow?

CYRUS
Yes please.
